    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best caulk for concrete is the one built for the direction of the joint. Flat driveway and patio joints want a self-leveling polyurethane or hybrid that flows flat and seals itself; walls, steps and foundation cracks want a non-sag gun-grade sealant that stays where you put it. Get that choice right, add backer rod where the joint is deep, and almost any of the six below will outlast the bargain-bin acrylic most people reach for first.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these sealants. This is a researched buying guide, and every figure on the product cards comes from the manufacturer's technical data sheet, label or product page. Where two versions of a sheet disagree, which happens more often than you would expect with sealants, we give both numbers and tell you which one to plan around.
          </p>

          <p>
            We started from the job rather than the brand. Concrete caulking jobs split cleanly into flat joints, where the sealant can flow and level itself, and vertical or sloped joints, where it has to stay where you put it. Inside each group we looked at movement rating, joint size limits, cure and traffic time, paintability and how forgiving the product is to apply. A sealant that is excellent but needs a primer, a perfect joint and a week of dry weather is not the right pick for most homeowners.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also kept this list distinct from our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> roundup. That page covers siding, trim, windows and general exterior gaps. Concrete is a different problem: the substrate is porous and alkaline, the joints are wider and deeper, and many of them carry foot or vehicle traffic. The products below are built for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we checked each product against the standards that actually mean something. ASTM C920 is the main specification for elastomeric joint sealants, and its class number roughly corresponds to the percentage of joint movement the sealant is rated to handle. Where a maker lists a class or a movement percentage, we quote it. Where we could not find one, we say so rather than guess.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="self-leveling-vs-non-sag" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Self-Leveling vs Non-Sag: Pick by the Joint, Not the Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Self-leveling sealant is thin enough to flow into a horizontal joint and settle flat on its own. You run a bead along the joint, it spreads to the sides, and gravity gives you a smooth, slightly recessed surface without tooling. That is exactly what you want in driveway expansion joints, sidewalk control joints and patio gaps, because a flat, recessed surface sheds water and does not catch shovels, tires or heels.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same property makes self-leveling sealant useless anywhere that is not flat. On a slope it runs downhill; on a wall it runs out of the joint entirely. Sika says plainly that its self-leveling sealant is not designed for sloped surfaces, and the same is true of every product in that category. If your driveway joint has a noticeable pitch, dam the low end with backer rod or switch to a non-sag product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Non-sag sealant, sometimes called gun grade, is thick enough to stay in a vertical or overhead joint. It goes in like a normal caulk and has to be tooled smooth with a putty knife or a gloved finger. Use it for cracks in foundation walls, the joint where a slab meets a wall, steps, block and brick, and any horizontal joint on a slope steep enough that a self-leveler would migrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            A lot of failed concrete repairs come down to using the wrong one. A non-sag sealant in a wide horizontal joint leaves a lumpy, hand-tooled surface that holds water and catches the edge of a shovel. A self-leveler on a step drips onto the next one down. Decide which kind of joint you have before you look at brands, and half the decision is made.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A quick rule</h3>

          <p>
            If you could pour water on the joint and it would sit there, use self-leveling. If the water would run off, use non-sag. For a mix of both, which is common on driveways that meet a garage slab, buy one of each rather than forcing one product to do both jobs.
          </p>
        </div>

