        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Polyurethane, Hybrid or Acrylic: What the Chemistry Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polyurethane is the traditional choice for concrete joints. It bonds well to masonry, it is tough enough for vehicle traffic once cured, and it stays elastic through freeze-thaw cycles. Four of our six picks are polyurethane. The downsides are handling and cleanup: it is sticky, it needs mineral spirits rather than water, and it is sensitive to moisture in the joint during application. Our comparison of <a href="/polyurethane-vs-silicone-caulk/">polyurethane vs silicone caulk</a> goes deeper on why silicone is a poor choice on concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrid polymer sealants, such as Quikrete's Advanced Polymer line, sit between polyurethane and silicone. They cure on contact with moisture in the air, generally tolerate damp surfaces better and tend to be easier to clean up while uncured. Quikrete lists 400 to 500 percent elongation for its self-leveling version. They are a good choice when you want polyurethane-like performance with fewer handling headaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acrylic latex sealants are water-based, easy to use and cheap, but they have far less movement capability than a good polyurethane or hybrid. Sashco Slab is the exception that earns its place, because it is formulated for concrete, textured to blend in and rated to stretch well beyond a basic acrylic. For a cosmetic crack it is a sensible choice; for an expansion joint that moves every season, it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Movement rating is the number to compare. A sealant rated for plus or minus 25 percent can handle a joint that opens and closes by a quarter of its width without tearing or losing adhesion. Expansion joints in a long driveway move noticeably between summer and winter, so a higher rating buys you real years of life. Sikaflex + Construction Sealant, at plus or minus 35 percent on Sika's 2026 sheet, has the most headroom of the products we could verify.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still unsure where caulk ends and sealant begins, our explainer on <a href="/caulk-vs-sealant/">caulk vs sealant</a> sorts out the terms. For concrete, you want something sold as a sealant with a stated movement rating, not a general-purpose painter's caulk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joint-design" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Joint Size, Depth and Backer Rod
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important number on a concrete sealant's data sheet is not the movement rating. It is the joint geometry. Every maker in this guide calls for roughly a 2:1 width-to-depth ratio, with the sealant between about 1/4 inch and 1/2 inch deep. A 1/2-inch-wide joint should get a 1/4-inch-deep bead; a 1-inch-wide joint should get a 1/2-inch-deep bead.
          </p>

          <p>
            That shape matters because sealant stretches best when it is wide and shallow. A deep, narrow bead cannot stretch without tearing, and a sealant stuck to the bottom of the joint as well as both sides, known as three-sided adhesion, tears even sooner. That is why the sheets insist on backer rod or bond breaker tape: it sets the depth and stops the sealant bonding to the bottom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backer rod is a round foam cord sold in diameters from about 1/4 inch upward. Buy it about 25 percent wider than the joint so it compresses into place, push it down with a blunt tool to the right depth, and run the sealant over it. Closed-cell rod is the type Sika specifies for horizontal joints; open-cell rod can hold water against the sealant, so check the data sheet for your product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width limits differ between products and are worth checking before you buy. DAP limits its self-leveling sealant to joints no wider than 1/2 inch. Quikrete's Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling is rated for joints up to 1 inch wide. If your old driveway joints are wider than 1 inch, consider a commercial joint sealant or professional repair, because a homeowner cartridge product is outside its design range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much to buy</h3>

          <p>
            Coverage depends almost entirely on joint size. Henkel's figure for Loctite PL Concrete Non-Sag is a useful benchmark: about 30.9 feet of 1/4-inch bead from a 10-ounce cartridge, dropping to about 13.8 feet at 3/8 inch. Wide driveway joints at 1/2 inch by 1/4 inch deep use sealant quickly, so measure the total joint length and buy a few extra cartridges.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Prep That Decides Whether It Sticks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most concrete caulk failures are adhesion failures, and almost all of them trace back to prep. The joint has to be clean, sound and dry. That means removing every trace of the old sealant, any loose concrete, weeds, dirt and dust. A wire brush, a utility knife and a stiff screwdriver handle most of it; a shop vac finishes the job. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">removing old caulk</a> covers the tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure washing helps, but it leaves water in the joint. Let the concrete dry fully before you seal, which usually means at least a day of dry weather, longer in cool or humid conditions. Polyurethane in particular reacts badly to moisture during application and can foam or lose adhesion. Hybrid sealants are more tolerant, but none of these makers recommend sealing into standing water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil and sealer residue are the hidden adhesion killers. If the slab has been treated with a penetrating sealer or has oil staining near the joint, clean the joint faces with a degreaser and rinse thoroughly. A sealant cannot bond to a surface that repels it. DAP notes that porous concrete may occasionally need a primer; if a test bead peels off easily after curing, prime before doing the whole job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, mind the temperature. Most concrete sealants should go on when both air and concrete are within the maker's stated range, typically above about 40 degrees and rising. Sika also advises installing self-leveling sealant when the joint is near the middle of its seasonal movement, which in practice means a mild spring or autumn day rather than the hottest or coldest week of the year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Apply Concrete Caulk Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            With the joint clean, dry and fitted with backer rod at the right depth, cut the cartridge nozzle at an angle to a size slightly smaller than the joint width. A good <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">caulk gun</a> with a high thrust ratio makes a real difference with thick polyurethane, especially in cold weather; a cheap gun will have you squeezing until your hand cramps.
          </p>

          <p>
            For self-leveling sealant, fill the joint in one continuous pass, aiming the bead at the center so it spreads to both faces. Fill to slightly below the surface, about 1/8 inch, so the cured sealant sits recessed and does not get scraped by tires or shovels. Do not try to tool it; let it level on its own. If a section is pitched, work from the low end up so the sealant does not run into areas you have not finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            For non-sag sealant, push the bead into the joint rather than dragging it along the surface, so the sealant is forced against both faces. Tool it within the skin time with a putty knife or a gloved finger dipped in soapy water, pressing it into the joint and leaving a slight concave profile. Tooling is not cosmetic: it drives the sealant into contact with the concrete and is a large part of what makes it stick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean up as you go. Polyurethane is far easier to remove before it cures than after, and once it skins over it smears instead of wiping off. Keep rags and mineral spirits nearby for polyurethane, and water for acrylic. Mask the edges of decorative or colored concrete with painter's tape if a clean line matters to you.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are tackling a larger driveway repair, our guide to <a href="/driveway-crack-repair/">driveway crack repair</a> covers what to do with cracks too wide or too unstable for sealant alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cure-and-traffic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cure Time, Traffic and Painting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete sealants skin over quickly but cure slowly, and the difference matters. Sika lists its self-leveling sealant as tack-free in about one to two hours with a final cure in three to five days, and recommends seven days before painting or immersion. Sashco lists Slab at a skin in 30 minutes, tack-free in two to four hours and a full cure in one to two weeks. DAP's self-leveling sealant is the quickest to tolerate rain, listed at about one hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foot traffic is usually fine once the surface is tack-free and firm, but give it longer than the minimum when you can. Sashco asks for at least 24 hours before foot traffic, or 48 hours in humid conditions. Vehicle traffic should wait for a meaningful share of the full cure, especially on a self-leveler sitting in a driveway joint. Block the joint off with cones or tape rather than relying on memory.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyurethane cures by reacting with moisture in the air, so cool, dry weather slows it down and warm, humid weather speeds it up. Deep beads cure from the surface inward, and Quikrete lists a cure rate of about 1/8 inch per 24 hours under standard conditions for one of its related products. A 1/2-inch-deep bead in cold weather can stay soft underneath for well over a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to paint, check the specific product. Sika lists its self-leveling and + Construction sealants as paintable with water-, oil- or rubber-based paints. Loctite PL Concrete is paintable only after full cure, and Henkel warns that rigid paints can crack over a flexible sealant. In practice, most homeowners leave concrete joint sealant unpainted and pick a gray or limestone color that blends in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not-to-caulk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When Caulk Is the Wrong Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealant fixes joints and cracks that move a little and need to be kept watertight. It does not fix concrete that is breaking up. If a crack is widening year after year, one side is higher than the other, or the slab is heaving or sinking, you have a structural or base problem, and sealant only hides it for a season. Our guide to <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/">concrete crack fillers</a> covers rigid patching options for non-moving damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Very wide gaps are another limit. Homeowner cartridge sealants top out around 1 inch of joint width at best, and most are rated for 1/2 inch. Anything wider needs a commercial joint system or a professional assessment. Filling a 2-inch gap with layers of caulk wastes material and fails early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some cracks also need a different material entirely. Active leaks in a basement wall respond better to hydraulic cement or an injection repair than to surface caulk. Spalled, flaking surfaces need resurfacing, not sealant. And cracks in asphalt, as opposed to concrete, need an asphalt-specific filler; concrete sealants are formulated for a rigid, alkaline substrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure why a joint keeps failing, our article on <a href="/why-does-exterior-caulk-keep-cracking/">why exterior caulk keeps cracking</a> walks through the usual causes, almost all of which apply to concrete as well. Fix the cause, and the sealant you choose from this list will last for years rather than months.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using self-leveling sealant on a slope</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Self-leveling sealant flows downhill. On a pitched driveway joint or a step it runs out before it skins over. Dam the low end with backer rod for a gentle slope, or switch to a non-sag polyurethane for anything steeper than a gentle pitch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping backer rod in deep joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every maker here calls for sealant no deeper than about 1/2 inch, with backer rod underneath. Filling a deep joint solid wastes sealant, slows curing and creates three-sided adhesion that tears as the joint moves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a damp joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyurethane reacts with moisture and can foam or lose adhesion if the joint is wet. Let a pressure-washed joint dry fully, often a full day or more, before sealing, and check the forecast for rain before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overfilling to the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bead flush with or above the slab gets scraped by tires, shovels and snowplows. Fill to about 1/8 inch below the surface so the cured sealant sits recessed and protected.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing acrylic for expansion joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Basic acrylic caulk has nowhere near the movement capability of a rated polyurethane or hybrid. In a driveway expansion joint it splits within a season. Save acrylic for cosmetic cracks and buy a rated sealant for joints that move.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

