          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For flat driveway and patio joints, a self-leveling polyurethane such as Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant is the default, rated for plus or minus 25 percent movement by Sika. For vertical cracks and walls, a non-sag polyurethane such as Loctite PL Concrete or Sikaflex + Construction Sealant is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use silicone caulk on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                General-purpose silicone is a poor choice on concrete. It often bonds weakly to porous masonry, attracts dirt and cannot be painted. Concrete-rated polyurethane or hybrid sealants bond better, hold up to traffic and are designed for the movement concrete joints see.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need backer rod for concrete caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for any joint deeper than about 1/2 inch. Makers including Sika, DAP and Henkel call for backer rod or bond breaker tape so the sealant stays between roughly 1/4 and 1/2 inch deep and bonds only to the two sides of the joint, not the bottom.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can drive on sealed concrete joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product and weather. Sika lists a three-to-five-day final cure for its self-leveling sealant, and polyurethane cures slowly in cool, dry conditions. Keep vehicles off for at least a few days and longer for deep beads or cold weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between self-leveling and non-sag concrete sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Self-leveling sealant flows flat into horizontal joints without tooling and suits driveways and patios. Non-sag sealant stays put in vertical or sloped joints and must be tooled. Use self-leveling where water would pool and non-sag where it would run off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint concrete caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many concrete sealants can be painted once cured. Sika lists its self-leveling and + Construction sealants as paintable with water-, oil- or rubber-based paints, while Loctite PL Concrete is paintable only after full cure. Rigid paints can crack over a flexible sealant, so test first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide a crack can concrete caulk fill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most homeowner sealants are rated for joints up to about 1/2 inch wide. Quikrete's Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling is rated up to 1 inch. Wider gaps need a commercial joint system or professional repair rather than layers of cartridge sealant.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

